                "retweet_count": 29,
                "source": "Twitter for iPhone",
                "text": "A nonsensical federal classification is making it harder for Americans with diabetes to get vital treatment. \n\nToday I introduced the ISLET Act to change it and save lives. \n\nHere\u2019s the situation:\n\nFor people living with Type 1 Diabetes, the immune system mistakenly attacks islet cells in the pancreas, which are responsible for producing insulin. \n\nIslets can be transplanted from deceased donors, allowing recipients to produce insulin naturally, reducing reliance on injections. Clinical trials have shown great improvement for patients with islet transplants. \n\nBut the Food and Drug Administration currently classifies islets as drugs, subjecting them to a host of regulations that do not apply to organ transplants, slowing down life-saving procedures and limiting the practice in the United States, while it has become a standard of care in other countries. \n\nThe Increase Support for Life-saving Endocrine Transplantation (ISLET) Act will correctly recategorize islets as organs, allowing widespread adoption of their medical use, and helping many Americans living with Type 1 Diabetes. \n\nRead more below!",
